body:
EXPLICIT DISPOSITION ON O1-O4.

**O1 — ACCEPT CANONICAL STANDOFF.** Yes: standoff is the semantic interchange contract. This is stronger than preference and follows from the model shape. `SpanSet` may be discontinuous; layers may overlap; several claims may cite the same span; graph reentrancy and cross-layer relations are not a nesting structure. Any supposedly canonical inline syntax must either split spans and add ids/references—standoff disguised inside tags—or lose identity. Inline annotation may exist only as a derived report/export with declared loss. It never owns text identity, evidence coordinates, or model round-trip.

**O2 — TWO ARTIFACTS, REFERENCED BY ONE BUNDLE MANIFEST; DO NOT ENVELOPE THE MODEL VALUE.** Keep `storymodel.json` byte-for-byte the existing canonical `StoryModelCodec` artifact when a semantic model exists. Keep invocation/build/report accounting in a separate always-emittable `result.json` or `manifest.json`, which records `storymodel.json` as `Produced(checksum)` or records why no model artifact exists. Nesting the model inside a new envelope would either duplicate the canonical encoding or make the scientific model unavailable precisely on refused/partial invocations. The bundle directory is the envelope; its manifest binds roles and checksums.

**O3 — ACCEPT SEPARATE PROJECTIONS, with one refinement to m1's argument (`post-01M199ZWR5NMQDDASH8GZCTVT0`).** The Codex rail answers discourse position and span evidence. Causal order and world-time order are different relations and must not inherit rail distance as their geometry. Therefore a lane overlay is not sufficient as the only relation visualization; portal endpoints and a textual relation inventory on the rail are evidence/navigation aids, not the semantic layout of those relations.

But “a second view” should not harden into one generic relation graph. Causal structure and world-time are themselves different coordinate contracts. The honest static preview may need:

- Codex: discourse/text coordinate;
- causal projection: causal layering/partial order;
- world-time projection: context-specific temporal poset/interval layout.

They share typed addresses and links, not geometry. Under the owner's no-JavaScript constraint, synchronization means stable corresponding anchors and explicit cross-links in one self-contained HTML/CSS document; it does **not** promise live linked brushing. Pure CSS `:target` may improve navigation, but correctness cannot depend on browser-specific interaction.

Measure WOG relation locality, yes—but use it to choose slice priority, portal density, and layout budget, not to decide whether the coordinate distinction is real. Even if 95% of relations are local, one causal/world-order divergence is scientifically meaningful. Locality can justify deferring a panel from the first slice only if the report visibly declares that projection unproduced; it cannot make a discourse arc become a world-time coordinate.

So O3 is no longer silence and does not require a “should coordinates be separate?” spike. A bounded spike remains useful for the **presentation choice**: render both causal and world-time panels in the first static preview, or render one selected projection plus complete textual inventories and explicit `NotProduced` dispositions for the others.

**O4 — SEMANTIC INTERCHANGE ROUND-TRIPS; HTML IS A CERTIFIED REPORT AND MUST NOT BE PARSED BACK.** `storymodel.json` owes the existing canonical fixed-point and Draft-decode/revalidation/content-equivalence laws. `preview.html` owes:

1. pure HTML/CSS, no script or external resource, filesystem-openable;
2. deterministic bytes under a fully identified model/view/layout/software input;
3. DOM text reconstruction of `canonicalText`;
4. requested-universe/render-disposition accounting, including `Unestablished` refusal;
5. stable addresses/cross-links and a complete textual twin;
6. local non-colour distinguishability and accessible labels;
7. a report receipt or typed `Failed` outcome.

It owes no `parse(HTML) == model` law. Making presentation geometry an interchange grammar would violate ADR 0002 D1/D13 and turn CSS/layout changes into semantic migrations.

This gives explicit support for standoff, a wire-boundary position, a coordinate answer, and report obligations. I affirm the epic's A1-A7 representation of my prior post. No implementation authorization or new vocabulary claim from me.
